2z13|n|A diseased condition of rye and other cereals, in which the grains become black, and often spur-shaped. It is caused by a parasitic fungus, Claviceps purpurea. ; The mycelium or spawn of this fungus infecting grains of rye and wheat. It is a powerful remedial agent, and also a dangerous poison, and is used as a means of hastening childbirth, and to arrest bleeding. ; A stub, like soft horn, about the size of a chestnut, situated behind and below the pastern joint. ; See 2d Calcar, 3 (b).	V:78g6,131,3~78g6,131,3	N:9r5,163,1;3ixz,147,6~3ixz,147,6;9r5,163,1
